Module visibility determines which declarations can cross a module boundary. Shared helper declarations are available within their owning module. Other modules can see only the names published by the applicable `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth` facade using `$export`.

### Shared helpers and public facades

A rooted directory is one module. Its ordinary `.moth` files, including files in unrooted subdirectories, share declarations through dependency clauses.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    @entry.moth
    components/
    ├── @mod.moth
    └── utils.moth
]

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/utils.moth
    internal_value #= 42
]

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/@mod.moth
    $export
    @utils internal_value
]

The facade imports and publishes `internal_value`. Another module can now use:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @components internal_value
]

Without `$export`, the same `@utils` dependency would be private to the facade. Other modules cannot bypass the child facade using `@components/utils`.

### Root-local declarations

Declarations authored directly in `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` or `@entry.moth` belong to that root's own scope. They are not shared with other roots or ordinary helpers. A facade can publish its own declarations, but a sibling root cannot import or re-export them.

Shared code belongs in ordinary helper files. Each root may depend on those helpers through normal dependency clauses. Same-spelling declarations in separate roots are independent unless an imported helper introduces a name collision.

Direct dependency paths to root filenames or `config.moth` are invalid. Depend on the module directory or a shared source file instead. See @../project-structure/#module-roots (Module roots).

### Separate public surfaces

`@mod.moth` publishes the normal module API. `@package.moth` publishes a scoped support package API or the external project package API at the project root. `@entry.moth` has no importable public surface and only its selected entry executes.

A dependency selects the surface appropriate to its consumer. It cannot fall back to the other facade. A child with only `@entry.moth` has no normal public API. Support packages have additional scoped consumer restrictions, described in @#project-local-packages (Project-local packages).

A facade with no `$export` declarations or clauses has an empty public API. A cross-module import of a private name is rejected regardless of the path used.

### Methods and public contracts

Receiver methods follow the visibility of their nominal receiver type. A caller with a value of a public receiver type can call its methods without separately importing that type name. Naming the type in a signature, constructor or conformance still requires a suitable type binding. Methods cannot be imported or exported individually.

Public signatures must expose only accessible types and trait evidence. Exported callables without an error slot must handle any escaping implicit built-in failure. The same guarantee applies to exposed methods and re-exports. See @../project-structure/#public-api (Public API).

### Dependency ordering

A dependency clause binds names throughout its file, independent of source position. File dependency cycles inside one module are allowed when declarations can be resolved in dependency order. Circular constant dependencies are rejected.

